 2. 🫎 Alaska gives Dems 56-seat Senate majority 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 


 Democratic U.S. Senate candidate Mary Peltola speaks during an election night rally in Anchorage on Nov. 3. Photo: Kerry Tasker/Reuters 🚨 10:19 p.m. ET last night, two weeks after Election Day: WASHINGTON (AP) — Democrat Mary Peltola wins election to U.S. Senate from Alaska.

 Former Democratic Rep. Mary Peltola's victory over two-term Republican Sen. Dan Sullivan flips another GOP-held seat in a state President Trump carried comfortably in 2024, Axios' Josephine Walker writes. 

 Why it matters: Peltola's win extends a historic Democratic midterm sweep, cementing a 56-44 Senate majority when the new Congress convenes in January. With 99% of votes counted, Peltola holds 50.1% of the vote to Sullivan's 45.8% — enough to win outright without triggering Alaska's ranked-choice voting process.

 She's the first Democrat elected to the Senate from Alaska since 2008. The race drew scrutiny after The New York Times reported allegations that Democratic operatives recruited another candidate named Dan Sullivan to draw votes from the incumbent. Via X 🖼️ The big picture: Peltola, a member of Alaska's Indigenous Yup'ik people, will become the nation's first Alaska Native U.S. senator, Axios' Russell Contreras writes. 

 Her path to victory ran through Alaska Native strongholds that delivered more than half of her statewide lead — a powerful moment for communities with a long history of disenfranchisement and forced displacement. 🏛️ The bottom line: Democrats flipped 9 U.S. Senate seat s, the most in nearly 70 years — Alaska, Iowa, Kansas, Maine, Mississippi, North Carolina, Ohio, South Carolina and Texas. 

 Dems last gained nine or more Senate seats in a midterm election 68 years ago, in 1958, during Republican President Dwight Eisenhower's second term. ( AP ) Go deeper : Communities behind Peltola's historic win. 

