 5. 💊 Scoop: White House drops FDA nominee 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Heidi Overton arrives for her Senate confirmation hearing on Capitol Hill in September. Photo: Tom Williams/CQ-Roll Call via Getty Images The White House is dropping Heidi Overton's nomination to lead the Food and Drug Administration because of a lack of congressional support, Axios' Alex Isenstadt has learned. 

 Why it matters: Overton's collapse shows how hard it is to find an FDA chief who can satisfy both the MAHA movement and the Senate. Zoom in: Overton, a physician and deputy director of the White House Domestic Policy Council, was nominated by President Trump in August.

 She faced criticism from segments of the MAHA movement that saw her siding with health and agriculture interests and helping undermine HHS Secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr.'s agenda. Antiabortion forces also questioned her commitment to tightening rules for prescribing the abortion pill mifepristone. Her nomination faced opposition from Democrats and skepticism from some Republican senators, including Sen. Bill Cassidy (R-La.), chair of the Senate's Health, Education, Labor and Pensions (HELP) Committee.

