 6. ⚠️ Terrifying rise of AI hacking tools 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Gabriella Turrisi/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Sam Sabin, author of Axios Future of Cybersecurity , writes about where AI is already helping hackers wreak havoc: 

 A new breed of widely available AI tools is helping hackers launch cyberattacks around the clock, breaching corporate networks in a matter of hours and overwhelming defenders.

 Why it matters: AI-enabled hacking is still in its infancy, but open-source tools — which are free to download — are fundamentally changing the economics of cybercrime. 🕵️ Zoom in: Recent attacks show how quickly these tools can help hackers penetrate corporate networks.

 The FBI warned that hackers used a tool called Artex to attack organizations worldwide — in some cases gaining powerful access to their systems in less than three hours. Separately, Amazon said hackers used Artex to steal AI model credentials, then used those resources to power additional attacks. Palo Alto Networks said Chinese-speaking hackers used a similar tool, Cairn, to launch 25 operations against at least 19 organizations. In a separate report Monday, Google said hackers used it during an intrusion into the U.S. aerospace sector. Share this story . 

 
 
